WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.200
Read Wiseman, Artemis II Commander, becomes JPL Deputy Director on November 30th. This is Fascinating Frontiers, Episode 237. Parallel moon and Mars plans from the United States and China, Virgin Galactics first Delta-class spaceplane, and Venus showing phases like the moon. Former astronaut Read Wiseman joins Caltech as Associate Vice President, according to the National Aeronautics and Space Administration, or NASA. His brief cover strategic leadership,

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:34.420
day-to-day laboratory operations, and national security programs as the Jet Propulsion

00:00:34.420 --> 00:00:40.680
Laboratory nears its 90th anniversary. He spent 165 days aboard the International Space

00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:47.280
Station on Expedition 41, then became NASA's Chief Astronaut. On Artemis II, he covered

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:55.040
695,081 miles during a lunar fly-by, farther than any humans before. Caltech President

00:00:55.040 --> 00:00:59.640
Ray J. Awardhanna called the appointment a natural fit for places that send spacecraft

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:01.680
to the far reaches of the solar system.

00:01:02.420 --> 00:01:08.220
Weisman commanded NASA's 21st Extreme Environment Mission Operations Expedition, living eight

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:14.360
days in an ocean floor habitat in 2016, and he holds a bachelor's degree in Computer and Systems

00:01:14.360 --> 00:01:19.920
Engineering from Rensselaer Polytechnic Institute and a Master's in Systems Engineering from Johns

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:21.120
Hopkins University.

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:26.420
NASA Administrator Jared Isaacman noted that Weisman has spent his career at the edge

00:01:26.420 --> 00:01:31.000
of what is possible and brings technical judgment and operational experience to push

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:36.220
that edge farther. Weisman said seeing the sun disappear behind the moon from deep space

00:01:36.220 --> 00:01:41.660
was the jumping off point to go do the impossible things that Caltech and JPL dream and do

00:01:41.660 --> 00:01:47.265
for NASA. The United States and China are accelerating hardware and mission timelines aimed

00:01:47.265 --> 00:01:50.805
at the moon, then crude Mars landings, according to Bloomberg.

00:01:51.505 --> 00:01:55.885
Those development paths include new landers, habitats, and propulsion systems.

00:01:56.465 --> 00:02:01.065
The two efforts run in parallel as both countries push for a lasting presence beyond Earth

00:02:01.065 --> 00:02:01.425
orbit.

00:02:02.349 --> 00:02:07.909
Virgin Galactic's new space plane, named Apuron after a worldwide public vote, flew from

00:02:07.909 --> 00:02:12.109
its Mesa factory to spaceport America on October 28th.

00:02:12.109 --> 00:02:16.909
Universe Today reports the ship carries an upgraded thermal protection system, and is built

00:02:16.909 --> 00:02:20.469
built for up to 500 missions, with a goal of flying twice a week.

00:02:20.969 --> 00:02:25.429
A second Delta-class craft is already being built and is expected to enter service in the

00:02:25.429 --> 00:02:27.329
second half of 2027.

00:02:28.049 --> 00:02:32.869
Apuron means boundless in ancient Greek, and the name was chosen from a pole that drew

00:02:32.869 --> 00:02:34.629
entries from more than 80 countries.

00:02:35.189 --> 00:02:41.189
The company plans to start flying paying customers again by mid-2027, after grounding the

00:02:41.189 --> 00:02:47.590
earlier VSS Unity vehicle. United Launch Alliance is targeting mid-November for the first Vulcan

00:02:47.590 --> 00:02:52.450
flight since a solid rocket booster nozzle problem, as reported by spaceflight now.

00:02:53.090 --> 00:02:58.150
That mission is set to carry 40 Amazon Leo satellites and will test repairs to the booster

00:02:58.150 --> 00:03:03.750
design. The heavy-lift rocket has sat grounded since the earlier anomaly and is now cleared

00:03:03.750 --> 00:03:11.819
to fly again with commercial payloads. Technicians finish the short-stack configuration of the

00:03:11.819 --> 00:03:16.659
Vehicle Assembly Building at Kennedy Space Center on October 28, NASA said.

00:03:17.199 --> 00:03:20.919
The core stage now sits mated with its boosters in that stacked arrangement.

00:03:21.559 --> 00:03:26.079
The work puts Artemis III hardware into a new assembly step inside the same building

00:03:26.079 --> 00:03:28.599
that prepares the vehicle for later rollout.

00:03:29.485 --> 00:03:35.465
NASA has committed to launching the ultraviolet explorer in 2031, Universe Today Reports.

00:03:36.125 --> 00:03:40.785
The telescope is expected to deliver roughly 100 times the sensitivity of the earlier

00:03:40.785 --> 00:03:43.625
Galax mission, which ended in 2013.

00:03:44.145 --> 00:03:49.365
It will run wide field ultraviolet surveys that sit alongside existing infrared maps of

00:03:49.365 --> 00:03:49.785
the sky.

00:03:50.445 --> 00:03:55.845
Those observations are meant to show new detail in hot stars, galaxies, and the interstellar

00:03:55.845 --> 00:03:56.205
medium.

00:03:57.411 --> 00:04:02.151
Beetlejuice's orbiting companion is pulling the red Super Giants outer layers into an egg-like

00:04:02.151 --> 00:04:02.531
shape.

00:04:03.051 --> 00:04:06.931
That gravitational tug helps account for the brightness changes seen from Earth.

00:04:06.931 --> 00:04:11.911
The companion alters the star's atmosphere in ways that change how the supergiant appears

00:04:11.911 --> 00:04:13.091
to vary over time.

00:04:14.011 --> 00:04:20.491
Web image dense gas clumps inside N159, a turbulent star-forming region in the large

00:04:20.491 --> 00:04:22.811
Magellanic cloud, according to NASA.

00:04:23.331 --> 00:04:26.371
The clouds sit 161,000 light years away.

00:04:26.951 --> 00:04:32.431
Lower metalicity there changes how stars assemble, so the region works as a nearby stand-in for

00:04:32.431 --> 00:04:33.771
early universe conditions.

00:04:33.771 --> 00:04:38.631
medicines. Reduced metal content also changes the cooling and fragmentation that break gas

00:04:38.631 --> 00:04:44.342
into new stars. Shock waves in the outer atmospheres of giant planets can strip away orbital

00:04:44.342 --> 00:04:50.662
energy and drive those worlds inward, according to simulations described by Fizz.org. A team

00:04:50.662 --> 00:04:55.242
at the Pennsylvania State University found the process at work in both Jupiter mass and

00:04:55.242 --> 00:05:00.702
Neptune mass planets, and the study appeared in the astrophysical journal. The shocks form

00:05:00.702 --> 00:05:03.942
in those outer atmospheric layers and bleed energy from the orbit.

00:05:04.402 --> 00:05:09.062
The result is a path that can park such planets on stiflingly close tracks around their

00:05:09.062 --> 00:05:09.522
stars.

00:05:10.530 --> 00:05:15.890
Chinese astronomers using ground-based observations have identified three previously unknown planets,

00:05:16.490 --> 00:05:17.670
the Global Times reports.

00:05:18.390 --> 00:05:22.770
The team is weighing what the finds mean for the stability of those systems and for long-term

00:05:22.770 --> 00:05:23.910
habitability models.

00:05:24.490 --> 00:05:29.150
The three worlds add to the catalog used to test how planetary systems form and change.

00:05:30.192 --> 00:05:35.152
Engineers are testing layered mylar sheets to control temperatures inside future moon habitats,

00:05:35.772 --> 00:05:43.112
universe-to-day reports. Those habitats must ride out extreme heat swings and still flex as they expand, compress and shift.

00:05:43.752 --> 00:05:48.052
The sheets have to stay flexible through that motion without giving up their insulating properties.

00:05:48.732 --> 00:05:55.292
The work tries to balance thermal protection against the mechanical demands of putting a habitat together and running it day-to-day.

00:05:55.292 --> 00:06:00.372
The same layers are meant to shield trained astronauts and later civilian residents from

00:06:00.372 --> 00:06:03.332
lunar conditions that sit far from Earth's mild range.

00:06:04.218 --> 00:06:08.978
Lithium-based passive detectors flew on the uncrewed Orion capsule during the Artemis-1

00:06:08.978 --> 00:06:13.358
lunar fly-by and recorded radiation doses at several body locations.

00:06:13.958 --> 00:06:18.338
The salt crystals store those long-term dose records without power or data lines.

00:06:18.938 --> 00:06:24.378
The Matrashka Astrorad radiation experiment used the detectors to map exposure across simulated

00:06:24.378 --> 00:06:29.961
human anatomy. China's long-march rocket family is being presented as carrying decades

00:06:29.961 --> 00:06:34.561
of engineering into new lunar and deep space work, according to Globe Newswire.

00:06:35.221 --> 00:06:39.241
The program points to reliability built across successive generations of vehicles.

00:06:39.821 --> 00:06:44.701
Those rockets are framed as the base for sustained exploration that continues from earlier flights.

00:06:45.719 --> 00:06:50.599
In brief, the Sun's outer magnetic field has been mapped in three dimensions for the first time,

00:06:50.599 --> 00:06:55.099
with researchers producing the first global estimates of that field beyond the visible

00:06:55.099 --> 00:06:58.819
surface and feeding those numbers into models of space weather drivers.

00:06:59.459 --> 00:07:07.279
Flexel Space and Satoro have booked two demonstration flights on Falcon 9 in 2027 and 2028 to qualify

00:07:07.279 --> 00:07:09.399
next-generation space solar cells.

00:07:10.330 --> 00:07:15.290
Venus swings between Earth and the Sun once every year and a half, lining up almost directly

00:07:15.290 --> 00:07:17.050
with our star from where we stand.

00:07:17.050 --> 00:07:22.210
In that alignment, the planet's night side faces us, and only a thin sliver of sunlit

00:07:22.210 --> 00:07:23.190
air stays in view.

00:07:23.710 --> 00:07:27.990
That sliver forms a glowing crescent whose tips stretch past a simple half-circle.

00:07:28.510 --> 00:07:33.270
Those stretched tips are the horns, made when sunlight slips through the dense upper atmosphere

00:07:33.270 --> 00:07:34.990
and bends around the curved limb.

00:07:35.590 --> 00:07:40.170
The same lineup that gives us a monthly new moon therefore gives Venus a new Venus phase.

00:07:40.850 --> 00:07:45.650
Venus's thick air scatters and refracts the light, so the crescent looks brighter and wider

00:07:45.650 --> 00:07:47.310
than geometry alone would allow.

00:07:48.050 --> 00:07:50.050
The new moon stays dark in that geometry.

00:07:50.670 --> 00:07:56.010
Venus' backlit atmosphere instead throws a glowing arc that can extend beyond 180 degrees.

00:07:56.690 --> 00:08:01.070
Ancient Babylonian astronomers wrote down the brightness changes thousands of years ago.

00:08:01.670 --> 00:08:06.390
The Maya kept similar records in their oldest surviving book, and other cultures logged the

00:08:06.390 --> 00:08:07.030
same cycle.

00:08:07.510 --> 00:08:12.370
The crescent can be caught with binoculars or a small telescope in twilight, and on rare

00:08:12.370 --> 00:08:16.190
air nights people with very sharp eyesight have seen it with no optics at all.

00:08:16.670 --> 00:08:21.410
Because the alignment repeats on a clockwork schedule, observers can say in advance when

00:08:21.410 --> 00:08:22.550
the horns will show again.

00:08:23.230 --> 00:08:29.150
An image from October 29th, 2026 shows Venus almost directly between the sun and Earth,

00:08:29.570 --> 00:08:33.150
the backlit air refracting and scattering sunlight into that glowing arc.

00:08:33.770 --> 00:08:38.570
The cycle is tied to the relative positions of Earth, Venus, and the Sun, so the carbon

00:08:38.570 --> 00:08:41.350
dioxide envelope keeps offering the same experiment.

00:08:41.350 --> 00:08:46.610
What still is not pinned down is how much that refraction changes with height and composition,

00:08:47.130 --> 00:08:50.390
and whether those shifts move with Venus' long-term climate cycles.

00:08:50.910 --> 00:08:55.850
We can watch the geometry unfold, yet the detailed light bending inside the horns remains

00:08:55.850 --> 00:08:56.850
an open problem.

00:08:57.410 --> 00:09:02.750
Future ultraviolet and infrared spectra from orbit could map where the bending happens, and

00:09:02.750 --> 00:09:06.910
how temperature and pressure gradients reshape the horns from one alignment to the next.

00:09:06.910 --> 00:09:12.292
Mid-November will test whether Vulcan's solid rocket booster nozzle repairs survive

00:09:12.292 --> 00:09:15.052
a flight carrying 40 Amazon Leo satellites.

00:09:15.552 --> 00:09:17.492
That's fascinating frontiers for today.

